Intraday Trading Platform – Test Status and Recommendations
1. Current Status: Phase Testing & Coverage Progress

The project has recently undergone a phase-by-phase testing execution, yielding a ~97.5% test pass rate overall. Out of ~615 tests run, about 19 tests failed, largely due to environment and mocking issues. Test coverage stands at only ~21% when measured sequentially (phases run separately), which is well below the historical ~60% target. The phase-wise outcomes were:

Phase 1: Foundation & Risk Management – Success. All core infrastructure tests passed (21 tests) with ~19% baseline coverage established. (20 tests were skipped, indicating some parts not yet executed in this phase.)

Phase 2: Alpaca Client Integration – Partial Success. ~50 tests passed, 10 tests failed. Failures stem from mocking conflicts with the Alpaca API – notably a MockOrderRequest used in tests has a mismatched constructor signature, causing TypeError exceptions. This indicates that the test doubles for external API calls are not aligned with the real API usage.

Phase 3: API Factory (FastAPI) – Success. 37 tests passed, 0 failed. FastAPI app startup, routing, and configuration were validated thoroughly.

Phase 4: WebSocket Manager – Success. 65 tests passed, 0 failed. Real-time communication (WebSocket server) behavior was confirmed, including connection handling and backpressure scenarios.

Phase 5: Feature Engineering – Success. 55 tests passed, 0 failed. The feature pipeline for the ML models is fully covered by tests, ensuring data transformations work as expected.

Phase 6: Model Manager (MLOps) – Success. 67 tests passed, 0 failed. The model management and registry components (loading, saving, model version control) are validated.

Phase 7A: Ensemble Model – Near Success. 82 tests passed, 1 test failed (and 1 skipped). The failing test is an error-handling scenario where an expected exception was not raised. In other words, the ensemble model code didn’t behave exactly as the test expected for a certain error condition. For example, earlier tests assumed a simpler weighting logic; in practice the ensemble was using a more complex confidence-weighted averaging, causing a mismatch with test expectations. This discrepancy required aligning either the implementation or the test (in this case the solution was to simplify the ensemble logic to match expected behavior).

Phase 7B: Additional Components (7B.1–7B.6) – Strong Success with Minor Issues. Over 200 tests covered various auxiliary modules (market data ingestion, social sentiment analysis, news, strategy logic, etc.). Most passed, with 8 tests failing across these combined sub-phases. The failures were minor, related to mocking in the sentiment analysis module and some edge-case handling. For instance, the social sentiment component tests had to simulate external APIs (Twitter/Reddit feeds), and a few tests failed due to incomplete mocking or unexpected data forms. Despite that, this phase significantly improved coverage for previously untested functionality – e.g. the social sentiment module went from 0% to ~77% coverage through new tests. A handful of edge-case tests also failed, indicating certain boundary conditions (like empty data or invalid inputs) are not yet handled as tests anticipate.

Overall, the systematic phase-by-phase approach has validated the core platform components individually. Most critical features (risk management, trading API integration, data pipeline, ML models, websockets, etc.) are now under test with high pass rates. The testing strategy proved effective in isolating components and incrementally increasing coverage. The platform’s test suite is nearing a point of stability, with the infrastructure deemed ready for further development (e.g. UI work) once the remaining issues are resolved. However, the current coverage (even when combining phases) is still far below the desired threshold – many parts of the codebase remain either untested or only partially tested, which we address below.

2. Areas Needing Improvement in Testing

Despite the progress, several gaps and issues in the test suite need to be addressed to reach full stability and higher coverage:

Mocking and External API Simulation: The Phase 2 failures highlight problems with how external API calls (Alpaca brokerage API) are mocked. The custom MockOrderRequest class used in tests did not match the actual Alpaca client’s usage, causing parameter mismatches. This needs fixing by adjusting the mock’s constructor or how it’s patched so it accepts the same parameters as the real MarketOrderRequest. In general, audit all mocks and test doubles for external services (broker API, social sentiment feeds, etc.) to ensure they faithfully mimic the real interfaces. This will prevent false failures due to interface differences. Additionally, consider using specialized HTTP mocking libraries (like respx or requests-mock) consistently across tests to simplify external API simulation. A standardized approach to mocking will reduce conflicts.

Test Environment Consistency: Ensure all tests run under a consistent configuration and do not interfere with each other. Some failures suggest environment/setup issues – for example, an authentication test is currently skipped due to a missing mock or bypass for auth tokens. Fixing this requires establishing proper test fixtures or setup for authentication (e.g. providing a dummy JWT or disabling auth in test mode) so such tests can run instead of being skipped. Likewise, make sure any global state (like environment variables, database files, or singletons) is reset between tests. Using pytest fixtures (with scopes like function or module) to initialize and teardown resources (database connections, config, etc.) will help isolate tests. The goal is that each test can run independently in any order without relying on others.

Handling of Edge Cases in Code: The few failing tests in Phase 7 (ensemble and others) indicate certain edge cases are not handled as expected. For instance, a test expected an exception in an ensemble model error scenario, but none was raised – meaning the code may be silently handling or ignoring an error that perhaps should propagate. Review those specific code paths to decide on correct behavior: if an error should be raised, modify the code to do so, or if the current behavior is acceptable, adjust the test to expect no exception. Consistency is key. Similarly, tests around sentiment analysis and other modules failing on edge cases suggests the code might not account for scenarios like empty inputs, null values, or API failures. It’s important to implement proper error handling and edge-case logic in the code (or adjust test expectations where the code’s behavior is deemed correct). Each fail is an opportunity to either harden the code or improve the test – resolve these so that all tests can pass reliably.

Alignment Between Tests and Implementation: A few failures were caused by tests that were written based on assumptions or outdated design rather than actual implementation details. It’s crucial to review tests for correctness: the test should reflect the intended behavior of the system. If a test expects a behavior that is not aligned with the design (as happened with the ensemble weighting logic), decide whether the implementation should change or the test should. In one case, the ensemble code was complex (confidence-weighted averaging) whereas the test expected a simpler approach, leading to failure. This was resolved by simplifying the code to match expectations. Moving forward, ensure that test specifications are updated when code changes, and vice versa, to avoid such mismatches. Tests are most useful when they codify the true requirements of the system.

Skipped and Fragile Tests: Identify tests that are skipped or marked xfail (expected failure) and address their underlying issues. For example, the skipped auth endpoint test mentioned above should be made to pass by providing the necessary mocking. Skipped tests indicate untested functionality; each skip should either be resolved or, if the test is obsolete, removed. Similarly, if any tests are flaky or timing-sensitive (e.g. some WebSocket or performance tests), improve their reliability by using timeouts or retrials as needed, or mark them appropriately (with @pytest.mark.slow or similar) so they don’t hinder routine test runs. The aim is a suite with no skipped tests and no unreliable tests, so that a full run’s results are trustworthy.

Test Data and Fixtures Management: The project includes many test data files (CSV price data, JSON responses, etc.). Ensure that the usage of these fixtures is efficient and correct. If tests are re-reading large CSVs or JSON repeatedly, consider using a module-scoped fixture to load them once and reuse the data across tests for speed. Also validate that test data is representative of real scenarios (e.g., the sample Alpaca responses JSON should cover typical and edge cases so the logic is fully exercised). Efficient handling of test data can improve test performance and coverage (since tests might be skipping certain validations due to lacking data for edge cases).

By addressing the above points, the failing tests can be fixed and the test suite will become more robust. The focus should be on making the test environment more predictable and aligning mocks and expectations with the real system behavior. This will set the stage for expanding coverage further in the next steps.

3. Framework Cleanup & Optimization

With a large number of test files and support scripts in the repository, it’s important to streamline the testing framework and codebase for maintainability and efficiency:

Organize Test Structure: The tests are currently divided by phases and areas (unit, integration, API, etc.), but there is some duplication and legacy naming (e.g. files like test_order_service_comprehensive.py and test_order_service_comprehensive_fixed.py both exist). It would help to consolidate or remove outdated test files to avoid confusion. Group tests in a logical structure (perhaps by feature or module rather than phase) now that the phased execution has served its purpose for initial validation. A clearer test directory structure will make it easier for developers (and tools like Copilot) to find relevant tests and understand coverage gaps.

Remove Obsolete Code and Artifacts: A number of markdown reports, old scripts, and backup files (e.g., .bak files, numerous MD status reports from each phase) are present in the repository. While these were useful during development, consider cleaning up files that are no longer needed to reduce clutter. Key documentation should be distilled into a few up-to-date documents (e.g. a final test report, a roadmap for UI phase, etc.), rather than keeping every intermediate report. This will prevent confusion and ensure everyone refers to the latest information. Version control history can preserve old data if needed. Similarly, any deprecated code (such as legacy implementations in legacy/ or unused modules) should be pruned to avoid wasting testing effort on dead code.

Improve Fixture and Plugin Usage: The project uses custom pytest plugins (tests/plugins/ like leak_guard, respx_compat, etc.) and helper modules. Review these for necessity and effectiveness. For example, if respx_compat.py is ensuring HTTP call compatibility for tests, verify it’s correctly applied in all relevant tests. If any plugin or fixture is not providing clear value, it could be removed to simplify the test runner configuration. Focus on using standard pytest features where possible (fixtures, marks) for clarity. Also, ensure that fixtures that set up common resources (database, app client, etc.) are defined in a conftest.py or dedicated module so they can be reused uniformly. This avoids repetitive setup code and makes tests cleaner and faster.

Optimize Test Execution Time: With hundreds of tests (and potentially more to be added), test runtime can become significant. Investigate ways to speed up tests without sacrificing coverage. This can include running tests in parallel using pytest-xdist (if tests are properly isolated, they should run on multiple cores). It may also involve marking truly slow tests (like long-running performance or load tests) so they can be excluded from normal runs. For example, if some Phase 7 tests involve heavy data processing or external API simulation with delays, label them and perhaps run them separately or less frequently. The goal is to keep the core test suite fast and convenient, encouraging regular use.

Memory and Resource Management: Ensure that tests clean up after themselves. For instance, if tests write to the database or to log files, those should be cleaned or use temporary locations. The presence of trading_platform.db in the repo suggests a database file – tests should probably use an in-memory SQLite or a temporary file instead, to avoid using a shared database state across test runs. If any tests spawn background tasks (async tasks, threads) or open network sockets, ensure these are properly closed at test end; the custom leak_guard plugin hints that resource leaks were a concern. Addressing these will not only prevent interference between tests but also improve test reliability and performance (no hanging connections or file handles).

Coverage Measurement Consistency: The coverage reporting should be configured to combine results from all phases. Currently, running phases separately under different pytest runs led to an understated coverage figure (~21%). To get an accurate picture, run all tests in one session or use coverage tools to combine data from multiple runs. Adjust the coverage settings (in .coveragerc or pytest ini) to include all modules and to omit any files that shouldn’t count (for example, if certain generated files or test scripts are being counted in coverage). A unified coverage report will highlight the true coverage (which might be higher – e.g., one comprehensive run showed ~48% coverage【27†】) and point out exactly which lines are missing. This is crucial for targeting new tests to write.

By performing this cleanup and optimization, the test framework will be leaner and easier to work with. New contributors (or AI assistants) will find it clearer to navigate. Efficiency improvements will also make it practical to run the full test suite frequently, which is important as you push toward very high coverage.

4. Roadmap to >95% Test Coverage

Achieving >95% coverage will require concerted effort to create tests for nearly all untested code, as well as improving existing tests. Below is a plan to reach this goal:

Identify All Untested Modules: First, use the coverage reports to pinpoint which files have little or no test coverage. From the latest data, there are several critical modules at 0% coverage – for example, the configuration loader, database models/repositories, core services like order FSM and position service, the broker integration layer, and parts of the ML pipeline (sentiment analysis, “noop” ML ops). Prioritize these for new tests. Start by listing out these modules and what their responsibilities are (e.g., backend/config.py – loads config and environment variables; backend/database/connection.py – sets up DB engine; backend/services/order_integrity_service.py – validates orders, etc.). This ensures no area is overlooked.

Write Unit Tests for Core Logic: Develop unit tests targeting the internal logic of each untested module. For instance:

Config & Settings: Verify that config parsing works (e.g., environment variables are read correctly, defaults apply, invalid configs raise errors). This can be done by temporarily modifying os.environ in tests and ensuring backend.config yields expected results.

Database Layer: If using an ORM or raw SQL, write tests for the repository functions (CRUD operations). These can run against an in-memory SQLite database to mimic production without side effects. The existing test_repositories_sqlite.py can be extended or replicated for all repository classes to ensure each method (create, read, update, delete) behaves correctly and handles edge cases (like unique constraints, missing records).

Broker/Alpaca Integration: Since direct calls to Alpaca are mocked, focus on testing the logic around it. For example, test that placing an order through the AlpacaClient properly formats the request and handles responses (simulate success vs. error responses using the mock). Also test fallback behaviors (like retry logic, if any). The goal is to cover both the happy path and failure paths of external calls.

Service Layer: Modules like order_fsm, positions_service, signal_service contain business rules that need validation. Write tests that feed in various scenarios and assert that the service returns the expected outcomes. For example, test the order finite state machine by simulating order events (placed, filled, canceled) and ensure it transitions states correctly and triggers the right side effects. These service tests can often be parameterized to cover multiple scenarios efficiently.

Strategies & Engine: For the strategy engine (strategies/engine.py and related strategy classes), create tests for each strategy logic – e.g., if there’s a momentum strategy, feed in a sample price series and check if the generated signal matches expectations. Strategy tests might use historical data from the fixtures (the CSV files) to simulate real market conditions and ensure the strategy outputs (buy/sell signals) are correct. This not only boosts coverage but also validates the trading logic.

Expand Integration and E2E Tests: High coverage isn’t just about unit tests – integration tests covering the interaction of components can hit many lines too. Leverage the existing end-to-end tests (like test_e2e_golden_path.py, pipeline tests, etc.) and add more scenarios. For instance, an end-to-end test could simulate a full trading day: ingest market data, run through feature engineering, generate model predictions, go through risk management, place orders (mocked), etc., to ensure the whole loop works. Such broad tests will naturally execute a lot of code, raising coverage. Ensure to cover both normal operation and failure scenarios in integration tests: e.g., what happens if the market data feed stops mid-day, or if the model raises an error – does the system recover or log appropriately? By testing these, you not only improve robustness but also execute error-handling branches that were previously untested.

Use Parameterized Tests to Increase Branch Coverage: Where functions have multiple code paths (branches), use pytest.mark.parametrize to feed combinations of inputs that exercise each branch. For example, if a risk check function behaves differently based on account balance, create test cases for low balance, high balance, negative, zero, etc. This systematic approach will help reach corner cases. Similarly, for the sentiment analysis module (now ~77% covered), add tests for the remaining branches – e.g., if there’s logic for when sentiment API rate limit is hit or when an empty response is returned, simulate those conditions. Filling in these gaps will push that module closer to 100%. The same goes for any conditional logic in the code; ensure every if/else and error path has at least one test scenario.

Incorporate Regression Tests for Fixed Bugs: Every time a test failure is fixed (like the ensemble logic or MockOrderRequest issue), add or update tests to prevent regressions. For example, after fixing the MockOrderRequest issue, include a test that calls the Alpaca order placement with minimal parameters to ensure it doesn’t raise a TypeError anymore. Similarly, after aligning the ensemble model’s behavior, have tests for both the simplified logic and a scenario that would have failed previously, confirming it now passes. These regression tests ensure that once you achieve ~95% coverage with all tests passing, it stays that way even as code evolves.

Continuous Coverage Monitoring: Integrate coverage checks into your workflow. For instance, configure the test runner or CI pipeline to fail if coverage drops below a certain threshold (incrementally raise this threshold as you approach 95%). Tools like Codecov or the pytest-cov fail-under setting can be used. This will enforce discipline that any new code must come with tests. As you near 95% coverage, each additional bit of untested code becomes noticeable. Leverage the coverage report’s line-by-line detail to write tests for those last remaining uncovered lines – often these might be error logging lines, or branches that are hard to trigger (like exception handlers). You might need to simulate error conditions (using mocks or monkeypatch to force an error) to hit those lines. By methodically targeting them, you can drive the coverage above 95%.

Real-World Scenario Testing: Finally, consider writing behavioral or scenario-based tests that mirror real trading workflows. For example, tests that simulate placing a series of trades under different market conditions, or tests that run the risk management logic against historical data to see if it correctly blocks trades. These not only increase coverage but also build confidence that the system will behave correctly in production. They can be structured as pytest test functions or even as standalone scripts that load sample data and run the platform’s functions end-to-end (ensuring no exceptions and expected outcomes). Make sure to validate outputs (like positions, P/L, logs generated) against expected results in these scenarios.

By following this roadmap, the project should incrementally increase coverage from the current ~48% (combined) towards the >95% goal. Focus on one module at a time, write thorough tests, and fix any uncovered issues those tests reveal. The key is thoroughness: nearly every function and code path should have at least one test. Given the complexity of the trading platform, reaching 95% coverage is ambitious but attainable with the above systematic approach. Each additional test not only bumps coverage but also fortifies the platform’s reliability, which is crucial before moving on to the UI phase.

Next Steps: Start by fixing the known test failures (Alpaca mock, ensemble exception, sentiment edges) so that you have a green test suite. Then proceed to add tests for the untested areas as outlined. It may help to use Copilot or similar tools to generate boilerplate tests for straightforward functions, but always review and validate that each new test is meaningful (covers a real scenario or checks a real requirement). Regularly run the full test suite (possibly with phases combined) to monitor coverage progress. With discipline and the above recommendations, you can achieve >95% coverage and a 100% pass rate, ensuring the platform is rigorously validated. This strong foundation will make the upcoming UI development and any future refactoring much more confident. Good luck!